   'time',
   'Brian honors U.S. Navy Special Warfare Operator Chief Petty Officer (SEAL) Brian R. Bill, 31, of Stamford, Connecticut, of an East Coast-based Naval Special Warfare unit. He died on Aug. 6, 2011, of wounds suffered when his unit''s helicopter crashed in Wardak Province, Afghanistan.

You complete three rounds of 5 rope climbs to 15 feet and 25 back squats (185 lb / 125 lb). That is 15 rope climbs and 75 back squats. The workout is short, but it is hard on both ends of your body. The rope climbs drain your grip and arms, and 25 squats at a heavy load each round take strong legs and a solid brace.',
   '[{"name": "Rope Climbs", "reps": "5", "tip": "Use a strong foot lock so your legs push you up. Come down under control."}, {"name": "Back Squats", "reps": "25", "tip": "Take the bar from a rack. Sets of 8 to 10 with short rests keep your form solid.", "rx_men": "185 lb", "rx_women": "125 lb"}]'::jsonb,
   'Record your finish time.',
   '["Rack the squat bar next to the rope", "Foot lock on every climb", "Plan your squat sets"]'::jsonb,
   '["Set the squat rack close to the rope so you can move straight from one to the other.", "On the rope, pull with your arms and stand up with your legs, keeping the rope between your feet.", "Shake out your hands after the climbs before you take the bar.", "Plan your squat sets, such as 10, 8, 7, and rest with the bar racked.", "Keep your chest up and your knees out on every squat, even late in the set."]'::jsonb,
   'Replace rope climbs with half climbs, lying rope pulls, or 3 pull-ups per climb. Lower the back squat to 135 lb / 95 lb, or use goblet squats. Cut the squats to 15 per round if needed.',
   '[{"question": "What is the Brian workout?", "answer": "3 rounds for time of 5 rope climbs to 15 feet and 25 back squats (185 lb / 125 lb)."}, {"question": "How many total reps are in Brian?", "answer": "15 rope climbs and 75 back squats."}, {"question": "What can I do instead of rope climbs?", "answer": "Half rope climbs, lying rope pulls to standing, or 3 pull-ups for every climb."}, {"question": "Who was Brian?", "answer": "U.S. Navy Special Warfare Operator Chief Petty Officer (SEAL) Brian R. Bill, 31, of Stamford, Connecticut. He died on Aug. 6, 2011, in Wardak Province, Afghanistan."}]'::jsonb,
